# Chooses an archive's NAME and its TOP-LEVEL DIRECTORY from the component
# being packaged (Phase 24 stage V).
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND IT IS A CPACK CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A CHOICE.
# The directory inside an archive is always `CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME`, in every
# case: component or not, and regardless of `CPACK_ARCHIVE_<COMPONENT>_FILE_NAME`,
# which renames only the .zip. That was measured, not assumed - a single cpack
# run over two components produced two archives that both unpacked into
# `TheStarsDontWait-0.1.0-win64/`, so a mod author who unpacked the game and
# then the tool got them MERGED, with the Forge's README, LICENSE and
# THIRD-PARTY.txt overwriting the game's and forge.exe landing inside the game
# folder. That last part is the promise `check_layout.cmake` guards, arriving
# through a file manager instead of through the build.
#
# So each package is its own cpack run with its own `CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME`,
# and this file is what sets it: `CPACK_PROJECT_CONFIG_FILE` is read at PACKAGE
# time, after `-D` arguments are applied, which is the only point where the
# component being built is known.
#
# ⚑ The version is `${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION}` and is deliberately NOT baked in
# at configure time (`@ONLY` above leaves it alone). Putting it in the package
# presets would have been the obvious route and would have hard-coded 0.1.0 into
# a JSON file that nothing updates when `project(... VERSION)` moves.

if(CPACK_COMPONENTS_ALL STREQUAL "forge")
    set(CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME "SolForge-${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION}-@solPackagePlatform@")
else()
    set(CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME "TheStarsDontWait-${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION}-@solPackagePlatform@")
endif()

# The .zip's own name, which CPack would otherwise suffix with the component
# ("...-win64-game.zip"). The game's archive filename is part of the game
# package and decision 010 says that package does not change.
set(CPACK_ARCHIVE_GAME_FILE_NAME "${CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME}")
set(CPACK_ARCHIVE_FORGE_FILE_NAME "${CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME}")
